{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/planning/IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md docs/planning/dependencies.md docs/planning/parity-matrix.md docs/planning/test-catalogue.md'","aggregated_output":"# Prisma AIRS Desktop v2 implementation plan\n\nStatus: product interview decisions D001–D023 accepted; D024/D025 settle source\nterms and all four architecture candidates; D026 makes Codex the independent\nchunk and phase reviewer. G00/G01 passed; implementation proceeds from C01. A passing plan review is not a passing architecture,\nSDK, application, live-tenant, or package review.\n\n## Outcome and exclusions\n\nDeliver a fresh macOS Apple Silicon desktop application with React presentation\nin Tauri, a focused Rust application core, direct Rust SCM SDK integration, and\nthe native Rust Harness engine. Academy supplies shared presentation and remains\nthe authority for remote learning. Preserve agreed working v1 behavior, using\nv1 as requirements reference. Do not translate its GPUI implementation wholesale.\n\nNo desktop billing, media-generation backend, local MySQL/Redis server stack,\nNode/Bun backend runtime, TypeScript workers, speculative browser client, or\nin-app updater is included. Academy gamification remains inside Academy. Gateway\nusage/cost/budget/limits remain operational product functionality. Build-time\nJavaScript tooling and React WebView JavaScript are expected.\n\nSource destination: `ssh://git@git-ssh.cdot.io/cdot/prisma-airs-desktop-v2.git`.\nCheckout: `/Users/cdot/development/redtail/prisma-airs-desktop-v2`.\n\n## Architectural guiding principles\n\n1. Design deep modules: substantial behavior behind a small domain interface.\n   Apply the deletion test; do not wrap every SDK method in a pass-through module.\n2. The interface is the test surface. Exercise production transitions and\n   adapters; use independent wire/protocol expectations for external contracts.\n3. One adapter is a hypothetical seam; two real adapters justify a seam. Shared\n   Academy presentation has real web/desktop consumers. Do not build speculative\n   browser/backend/plugin architectures for this desktop delivery.\n4. Rust owns authority and locality for tenant/credential/workspace context,\n   request lifecycle, configuration review, protected storage, and approvals.\n   React owns presentation and editable input, not permission to execute.\n5. The SDK owns its declared transport/authentication contracts. The application\n   owns interactive identity, OS storage, context admission, and workflow policy.\n   Do not duplicate SDK retry/pagination/OAuth policy without a documented need.\n6. Unknown outcomes and partial/stale/denied/unsupported data remain distinct.\n   Never map an unknown verdict to allow or a cancelled write to successful rollback.\n7. Host credentials and capabilities are explicit. No ambient environment-file\n   discovery, inherited production fallback, generic renderer secret access,\n   unrestricted native plugin grants, or cross-origin credential forwarding.\n8. Shared appearance does not imply shared learner state. Tenant operations,\n   Academy identity/progression, Harness inference identity, and MCP grants stay\n   separately scoped. A tenant switch does not sign the learner out of Academy.\n9. Keep provenance and reproducibility local to dependency selection. Exact\n   versions, source hashes, build inputs, notices, and protocol pins are evidence;\n   a neighboring active worktree is not a release dependency.\n10. Measure native usability/performance and the actual installed package. A\n    successful browser render or mocked IPC assertion is insufficient evidence\n    for Tauri host behavior, VoiceOver, Keychain, sidecar isolation, or notarization.\n\nConcrete interfaces, crate decomposition, shared package extraction, and storage\nformats are outputs of G01. This plan defines behavior and testable work, not\nunreviewed Rust signatures or a predetermined hierarchy of tiny modules.\n\n## Ordered phases and chunks\n\nEvery Cxx has its own instruction file under [chunks](chunks/README.md). Complete\nchunks in numeric order. A dependency delay blocks its chunk; report it honestly\nand continue only research/preparation explicitly within that chunk. Do not\nsilently advance a later phase or replace a real dependency with a fake to pass.\nFor a prolonged upstream stall, report the exact dependency and offer an owner\nreorder/scope decision. Do not wait indefinitely or reorder silently; an approved\nchange updates predecessors, plan and review evidence before execution.\n\n| Phase | Chunks   | Result                                                           |\n|-------|----------|------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| P00   | G00, G01 | Confirmed plan; selected and reviewed architecture               |\n| P01   | C01–C03  | Shared desktop shell, native core, first complete Runtime scan   |\n| P02   | C04–C07  | Academy identity, learner presentation, assessment, offline work |\n| P03   | C08–C12  | Runtime/DLP administration, Sessions, reports, Library           |\n| P04   | C13–C15  | Existing Red Team/Model reads and complete API Client workflows  |\n| P05   | C16–C18  | Gateway workspace/configuration, reviewed changes, inference     |\n| P06   | C19–C22  | Native Harness, Rust sign-in, typed host tools, workspace MCP    |\n| P07   | C23–C24  | Reviewed imports, native quality, accessibility/privacy closure  |\n| P08   | C25–C26  | Controlled live qualification; signed installed DMG acceptance   |\n| P09   | C27–C44  | UI v2 \"Refined Native\": spec, shell, wizard, destinations (D058); re-cut from C35 as one screen per chunk ported from the prototype (D059) |\n\n### G00 — Plan confirmation\n\nFinish the decision ledger, baseline coverage, chunk instructions, review/test\nprotocols, dependency gates, and architecture handoff. Obtain an actual independent\nplan review meeting D019. Product decisions are accepted and the owner explicitly\nselected the architecture candidates and source terms in D024/D025. Continue the\nrequested design work; no further blanket scope question is needed. Architecture\nreview and implementation evidence remain distinct gates.\n\n### G01 — Architecture improvement workflow\n\nFollow [the handoff](architecture-handoff.md) and the requested\nimprove-codebase-architecture skill. Explore the named areas, show a temporary\nHTML report with before/after candidates, ask the owner to choose, and grill the\nselected design. Produce ADRs, interface/test ownership, dependency/import\ndecisions, and concrete chunk command specifications. Review the architecture\nat least 9/10 in every critical dimension and overall. Revise this plan if the\nselected design affects it, then re-review affected planning evidence.\n\nNo application scaffolding, shared-source refactoring, real-data migration,\nproduct endpoint calls, or packaging implementation begins before G01 passes.\n\n## Per-chunk execution contract\n\n1. Read AGENTS/CLAUDE, glossary, accepted ADRs, the chunk, predecessor receipts,\n   dependency manifest, test catalogue, and its canonical implementer prompt.\n2. Confirm a clean approved source snapshot and identify unrelated user work.\n   Resolve dependency availability/provenance and baseline check limitations.\n3. Implement only that chunk and approved supporting changes. A shared Academy,\n   SDK, or Harness change needs its own reviewed revision and regression evidence;\n   preserve active work and do not move another project's main branch casually.\n4. Run exact checks from the approved command manifest. Use deterministic local\n   fixtures and literal external contracts, then the appropriate native tests.\n   Never call hosted application endpoints by accident.\n5. Record commands, environments, inputs, results, failures, hashes, limitations,\n   and rollback rehearsal. Save raw sensitive evidence privately; commit redacted\n   summaries with references/hashes, not credentials or customer bodies.\n6. Freeze source and dependency inputs. Request actual independent Codex\n   review through [the independent prompt](prompts/review-chunk.md) (D026).\n7. If any critical dimension/overall score is below 9, required fixes remain,\n   source changed, or mandatory evidence is absent: remediate, rerun affected\n   checks, freeze a new snapshot, and request a new review of the same chunk.\n8. Complete only after the receipt and evidence validate against current inputs.\n   After a phase's last chunk, obtain the cumulative Codex phase review\n   ([prompt](prompts/review-phase.md)) before starting the next phase.\n   Make a focused commit; revalidate that its tree matches the reviewed tree.\n   Later edits invalidate affected receipts and trigger targeted regression\n   checks/re-review. Preserve failed attempts; never pressure a reviewer to pass.\n\n## Command contract to establish in C01\n\nThese commands are a required future interface, not tools that exist today:\n\n```text\ncargo run --locked -p xtask -- check --chunk Cxx --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json\ncargo run --locked -p xtask -- test --chunk Cxx --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json\ncargo run --locked -p xtask -- native --chunk Cxx --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json\ncargo run --locked -p xtask -- review --chunk Cxx --reviewer codex --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json\ncargo run --locked -p xtask -- review --phase Pxx --reviewer codex --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json\ncargo run --locked -p xtask -- complete --chunk Cxx --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json\ncargo run --locked -p xtask -- package --kind preview --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json\ncargo run --locked -p xtask -- package --kind release-candidate --manifest docs/architecture/checks.json\n```\n\nC01 creates the controller and a versioned manifest of exact commands, working\ndirectories, targets, features, fixtures, timeouts, and expected evidence. It\ncannot implement the later chunks itself. G01 specifies actual Rust/pnpm/Tauri\ncommands after choosing the workspace; a future engineer must not guess them or\nclaim these placeholder commands already ran. Document/test the controller's\nrejection of missing, stale, malformed, edited, or sub-threshold receipts.\n\n## Coverage and evidence\n\n- [Feature baseline](feature-baseline.md): existing working/preview/stub behavior.\n- [Parity matrix](parity-matrix.md): work ownership and release closure.\n- [Dependency gates](dependencies.md): accepted inputs and unresolved qualification.\n- [Test catalogue](test-catalogue.md): named behavioral/native/artifact test cases.\n- [Review protocol](review-protocol.md): scoring, receipt fields, invalidation.\n- [Release contract](release-contract.md): live scope, signing, package/install proof.\n- [Mandatory live checks](live-checks.md): fixed L-family/F-smoke requirements and\n  owner-approved per-workflow live scope; no access-driven silent reductions.\n- [Distribution gate](distribution.md): owner-selected source/binary terms,\n  Academy permission, framework disposition and exact shipped notices.\n- [Status](status.md): authoritative current state; planned is not implemented.\n\nG01 must convert each chunk's named tests into exact invocation commands and\nobservable assertions. Tests cannot simply repeat implementation decisions.\nFresh signed-artifact checks bind the final DMG hash and installed bytes; source\nor fixture checks do not substitute for them.\n\n## Expected sequencing dependencies\n\nThe scan proof can use accepted SDK Phase 3 after native macOS qualification.\nManagement/DLP uses accepted Phase 4 or a later explicitly reviewed compatible\npin. Existing Red Team/Model reads need verified Phase 5 capabilities; complete\nGateway needs Phase 6 capabilities. Harness/Academy transports are application\nwork, not delivered by the SCM SDK. Keep these upstream dependencies visible and\ndo not require unrelated SDK operations merely because they share a library.\n\nFirst-slice metrics establish named budgets before C04. Every UI chunk includes\nkeyboard/focus checks. Later introducing chunks register their numeric workload\nbudgets before implementation/acceptance measurement under T20 governance; budget\nchanges need recorded rationale and owner or independent reviewer acknowledgement.\nC24/C26 compare these pre-registered values, not newly fitted limits. C24 closes\nthe full native/VoiceOver/performance and cross-feature offline matrix.\nPreview packages may demonstrate accepted scope but must name missing features.\nOnly C26 can establish stable installed-package readiness.\n\n## Completion and delivery\n\nStable v2 requires every agreed working-v1 parity row closed, all chunk/phase\nreviews passing, designated live acceptance complete, no missing mandatory\nchecks, and C26's installed signed/notarized DMG evidence. V1 stays available;\nfresh credentials and reviewed imports remain explicit. Updates initially use\nmanual signed DMGs with rollback instructions.\n\nThe active implementation task may deliver reviewed Git commits to the exact\nowner-specified remote using normal pushes. Application release publication,\nhosting changes, and promotion over v1 must remain within the owner's explicit\nrelease instruction; preparing a signed package does not by itself publish it.\n# Dependency and source gates\n\nArchitecture proposed exact first-slice versions in docs/architecture/qualification.md.\nAs of C01 (2026-10-17) the desktop graph is resolved, locked and compiled on\nthe macOS ARM64 host; the resolved inputs are recorded below. Later SDK\nfamilies and other inputs remain gated at their consuming chunks.\n\n## Source ownership\n\n| Input          | Inspected revision                       | Ownership/use                                           |\n|----------------|------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------|\n| cdot-framework | e3553b27a3d6bf457a2e585c5ae479c7db89a17b | Selective patterns/modules after provenance evaluation  |\n| Desktop v1     | 74754c02b19d87187b6dca81f64550a669b0f841 | Behavioral/contracts reference; preserve checkout       |\n| Academy        | 7f3a28c4082a5bb278bbff5d79fab106c240155a | Shared React source; hosted learning authority          |\n| Native Harness | 9331a5839bc6a763d94dee7584746f58bbbe1181 | Desktop-capable native engine/protocol input            |\n| SDK Phase 3    | ec6230d2a19b9c835c9e4c9487795b38ad538af3 | Accepted scan subset; native/live qualification pending |\n| scm-sdk crate  | crates.io 0.1.0, sha256 6d555d955c3734dd5ee78f7173569bdecd4426095b2ffedc4b77d8f0f2c2e8d9, from 4f65dc48686a3b701ed0585812fcca46859f7e5a | D027 scan input from C02 (scan feature only); 164 package unit tests pass natively; live qualification is C25 |\n| SDK Phase 4    | 32cbcb6d9b04478aeca21e767ed6ddb8949ec7bb | Accepted management/DLP/IAM subset; qualify exact uses  |\n\nDo not consume an active neighboring worktree via an unpinned path and later\nclaim the accepted commit was built. Obtain a clean revision-specific source\nsnapshot/package without secrets or ignored local configuration. Manifest both\nits origin revision and actual source/package hash. A dependency update reopens\nthe affected contract/native/artifact checks and review. Never fabricate SDK\nmethod parity from a similar TypeScript method name.\n\n## C01 resolved inputs\n\nRecorded by C01 and bound to its review. Locks: `Cargo.lock`, `pnpm-lock.yaml`,\n`rust-toolchain.toml`, `docs/architecture/toolchain.json`,\n`docs/architecture/inputs.lock.json`; the controller's dependency fingerprint\nhashes them with the consumed `.inputs/` bytes and tool identities.\n\n| Input | Resolved | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Rust toolchain | 1.99.0 (b940084d7 2026-09-28) | Corrected from 1.90.0; proc-macro dylib dyld failure at deployment target 13.0 on macOS 27 (evidence/C01/toolchain-correction.json). Release `strip` removed for the same failure. |\n| Tauri / tauri-build / Tauri CLI / @tauri-apps/api | 2.12.4 / 2.7.3 / 2.12.3 / 2.12.4 | `custom-protocol` added by the Tauri CLI for bundles |\n| ts-rs | 12.0.1 (serde-compat, no-serde-warnings) | Generated declarations committed; `xtask bindings --check` |\n| React / ReactDOM | 19.2.3 | Single instance per consumer (pnpm strict peers) |\n| Tailwind / Vite / Vitest / plugin-react / TypeScript | 4.3.2 / 7.3.6 / 3.2.6 / 5.2.0 / 5.9.3 | Matched to Academy's resolved lock instead of the earlier 4.1.18 / 7.3.0 proposal |\n| Build Node / pnpm | 24.21.0 / 10.6.5 (corepack shim in `.tools/bin`) | Build-only; never shipped |\n| Academy shared presentation | `ai-security-academy` 891f65eef76b53eb87155d0f0f5622228e217a2d on branch `v2/desktop-presentation` (base 7f3a28c4) | Pushed to the Academy origin on 2026-10-17 (owner-approved; main untouched). `xtask fetch-inputs --source <academy clone>` materializes `.inputs/academy` (tree sha256 1c4ce702…); a fresh clone from origin reproduces it (evidence/C01/clean-checkout). |\n\nThird-party notices for the shipped graph are in `docs/architecture/notice-inventory.json`\n(`xtask notices --check`). The Academy package is Academy-owned and recorded by\nits pinned commit, not relicensed.\n\n## Required gates\n\n- **Distribution terms:** G01 records owner-approved v2 source/binary terms,\n  Academy redistribution permission and every framework-derived import's\n  disposition under [distribution.md](distribution.md). No extraction before\n  clearance; C26 verifies notices/terms against exact shipped inventory.\n\n- **Provenance:** evaluate each direct import's actual license/notice and allowed\n  usage. cdot-framework is not blanket MIT and its billing-retention condition\n  cannot be erased by renaming. Prefer independently implemented patterns where\n  direct copying is unsuitable. Academy sharing is owner-directed; establish\n  package ownership and third-party notices rather than inventing a license.\n- **Academy packaging:** React19/Tailwind4/Vite7 are observed in Academy, while\n  the framework uses React18/Tailwind3. Select a compatible pinned desktop stack\n  and one shared presentation source. The existing UI package re-exports app\n  source and is not a ready independently reusable full learner app.\n- **Shared-source changes:** propose the smallest extraction that supports actual\n  web/desktop consumers. Keep Academy web behavior and hosted contracts stable;\n  run both projects' affected tests. Pin the reviewed shared revision/package\n  before completing its consuming desktop chunk.\n- **SDK scan:** ec6230d2's accepted subset supplies synchronous scan. It requires\n  explicit scan credentials; management OAuth is separate. Accepted Linux\n  reports do not establish macOS ARM64 support. Qualify the native compiler/TLS\n  dependencies, runtime integration, scanner contracts, cancellation, sanitized\n  errors, and unknown outcomes in C02–C03.\n- **SDK administration:** 32cbcb6d's accepted Phase4 is a candidate input for\n  C08–C12. Map every actual operation, option, pagination bound, and error to\n  accepted source/tests. SDK family labels are insufficient proof of parity.\n- **SDK later families:** Phase5 Red Team/Model and Phase6 Gateway were developing\n  or pending during inspection. Their exact accepted pins are prerequisites to\n  C13/C16–C18. Do not consume unfinished work or substitute mocks as completion.\n  Stable v2 must qualify the SDK subset it actually uses, including native/live\n  evidence; it need not claim every unrelated SDK method is complete.\n- **Harness:** retain the desktop-capable native engine/protocol after source and\n  package checks. Do not substitute an older canonical checkout or npm launcher.\n  Rebuild/reseal v2 resources to omit Node/managed CLI where proven unnecessary;\n  old v1 resource hashes do not attest the changed v2 bundle. Preserve inference\n  and MCP routing/context/tool restrictions.\n- **Native host:** select pinned Tauri2/Rust/toolchain dependencies compatible\n  with macOS ARM64 and the chosen SDK. Resolve one application-owned async\n  runtime strategy; do not create an independent runtime per request.\n- **Identity:** specify independent v2 bundle/display/data/Keychain identities in\n  G01. Test v1/v2 coexistence, reset, uninstall, import, and rollback. Do not\n  silently reuse a v1 namespace or change the learner's remote account.\n- **Live environment:** identify designated test tenants/accounts, scopes,\n  reversible resources, budgets, and cleanup/readback before C25 execution.\n  Prod configuration discovery is not mutation authorization. Missing access is\n  a real dependency gap; never report skipped tests as passed.\n- **Hosted OAuth registrations:** G01 maps reuse/new-client/redirect/scope needs\n  for Academy device authorization, Harness company SSO and MCP. Responsible\n  Academy/Gateway/IdP operators own authorized staging/configuration changes and\n  deployment. V2 identity alone neither proves a new registration is required\n  nor authorizes production changes. Resolve before the consuming live tests.\n- **Signing:** identify an authorized Developer ID, provisioning/entitlement\n  requirements, notarization access, and a native acceptance host. Access secrets\n  only at use time through approved storage, never committed plan variables.\n\n## Official host references\n\nTauri supports Rust commands and async messages between presentation and host;\nthe selected command/module interface still requires G01 design and review.\n[Calling Rust](https://v2.tauri.app/develop/calling-rust/)\n\nWindow/WebView capability grants require deliberate scoping; renderer grants do\nnot replace Rust's application authority checks.\n[Capabilities](https://v2.tauri.app/security/capabilities/)\n\nDMG distribution and macOS signing are separate from successful compilation.\n[DMG packaging](https://v2.tauri.app/distribute/dmg/),\n[macOS signing/notarization](https://v2.tauri.app/distribute/sign/macos/).\n# Working-v1 parity and release closure\n\nAll v2 rows are **planned**. No v2 source/native/live/artifact result exists yet.\nThe baseline is [feature-baseline.md](feature-baseline.md); do not expand a\npreview/stub into required live behavior without an explicit scope decision.\n\n| Requirement family                     | Owning chunks   | Closure requirement                                                                  |\n|----------------------------------------|-----------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Tenant setup/scan-only/protected state | C02,C03,C08,C23 | Context/capabilities, reviewed endpoint overrides/profile import, verification/reset |\n| Runtime synchronous scan/cases         | C03,C12         | Faithful verdicts, profiles, cancellation and no silent replay                       |\n| Profiles/topics/applications/keys      | C08,C09         | Exact supported reads/writes, review binding, one-time secrets                       |\n| DLP/deployment catalogue               | C08,C10         | Exact supported operation subset and real multipart semantics                        |\n| Sessions/content/search/cache          | C11             | Explicit reveal, protected persistence, bounded completeness                         |\n| Metadata FP/FN reports                 | C11             | Edit/remove/undo/export without suppression side effects                             |\n| Home/Inbox/source observations/charts  | C12,C18         | Runtime and Gateway data, freshness/partial/unknown/attention                        |\n| Five-product Library/specifications    | C12             | Authoring/import/export; only supported Runtime execution                            |\n| Red Team read-only observations        | C13             | Accepted actual reads; no invented live execution                                    |\n| Model/skill read-only observations     | C13             | Accepted actual reads and partial-source disclosure                                  |\n| API Client documents/projects/imports  | C14             | Fresh-input reconciliation, drafts/conflicts/secrets/Git adoption                    |\n| API Client transport/history/catalogue | C15             | Real HTTP/cookies/downloads, scoped auth, no mutation bypass                         |\n| Gateway workspace/config/admin subset  | C16,C17         | Supported scope/options and reviewed multi-step outcomes                             |\n| Gateway inference/logs/telemetry/cost  | C18             | Streaming, route/model selection, operational budgets/limits                         |\n| Harness native run/stream/lifecycle    | C19             | Real Rust engine/protocol, cleanup and no packaged Node runtime                      |\n| Harness identity/protected history     | C20             | Rust SSO/key lifecycle, scope-safe restore/export/delete                             |\n| Harness typed tools/project permission | C21             | Investigation/proposals and enforced existing sandbox restrictions                   |\n| Workspace MCP                          | C22             | Rust auth/grants, exact call approvals and execution evidence                        |\n| Academy identity/account isolation     | C04             | Device OAuth, scope admission, tenant-independent learner identity                   |\n| Academy learner presentation           | C05             | Shared catalog/lessons/checks/sandbox/UI; feature-local gamification                 |\n| Academy Program/exams/certificates     | C06             | Hosted access/timing/grading/award authority and account workflows                   |\n| Academy encrypted offline work         | C07             | Lease/revision/account-aware reading/drafts; explicit submission                     |\n| Reviewed imports/fresh authentication  | C23             | User work, source preservation, restart/rollback; no old approvals                   |\n| Native UX/privacy/measured performance | C01–C24         | Per-chunk checks plus cumulative actual native quality closure                       |\n| Controlled live behavior               | C25             | Designated scope, real outcomes, readback/cleanup, honest gaps                       |\n| Signed installed package/update        | C26             | DMG/notary/Gatekeeper, exact bytes, fresh user, coexistence/rollback                 |\n\n## Required per-workflow ledger\n\nThe [workflow ledger](../architecture/workflows.md) expands these families into\n66 operation/workflow classes. C01 records machine-readable evidence rows and\nconsuming chunks map exact supported operations/options before implementation.\nEvery row needs an ID, baseline source, current classification, responsible chunk,\nexact SDK/shared dependency contract, and independent expectations. Preserve four\nevidence categories separately: source implementation, fixture/contract results,\nnative/live qualification, and final installed-artifact qualification. Record\ndenied/unsupported/partial results honestly rather than collapsing them to pass.\n\nG01/C01 adds a live_requirement (required/not_required with reason) and separate\nready/blocked-by-access status to every row under [live-checks.md](live-checks.md).\nThe owner approves the expanded list before C25. Missing access cannot shrink\nmandatory scope; a reduction requires a recorded owner decision and new review.\nT23 cross-feature offline acceptance applies to local work and pending operations,\nnot just Academy. C24 closes that cumulative matrix.\n\nV2 source completeness alone does not close a row. Each workflow needs applicable\nsuccess and failure assertions, context/authority coverage, native acceptance and\nrequired live/artifact evidence. C25/C26 review the cumulative ledger against\nthe final frozen input set. A required blocked check blocks stable completion.\n\n## Intentional non-goals and retained classifications\n\n- Financial desktop billing/monetization and media-generation infrastructure are\n  excluded. Remote operational cost/limit administration is retained.\n- Exception Patterns remains explicitly synthetic/inert if shown. FP/FN reports\n  do not suppress scans or modify policy.\n- New Red Team execution and dedicated Model Security execution remain outside\n  parity. Their existence in the new SDK does not authorize adding product scope.\n- Academy admin/assessor operations remain web-only. Learner feedback with an\n  administrative namespace is classified by actual authority, not string matching.\n- No browser app, in-app updater, or additional supported OS/architecture is\n  required for first stable v2.\n- Shared Academy appearance replaces the GPUI presentation implementation. Do\n  not infer a pixel clone or identical internal widget/theme catalogue. Preserve\n  theme preference workflows and specify reviewed mappings for imported settings.\n- The generic SCM workbench catalogue does not prove all 193 requests were live\n  tested. Classify its supported execution/admission separately from dedicated\n  workflows and bind its request metadata to a reviewed source snapshot.\n\nManaged scan/identity HTTP deliberately defaults to Direct rather than v1's\nambient system proxy behavior. On mandatory-proxy networks, onboarding/settings\nshows Direct/System/Explicit policy and requires operator selection; a failed\nfirst request must surface proxy setup rather than an unexplained regression.\nGeneric workbench remains System by default. C02/C15 qualify all modes.\n# Behavioral and native test catalogue\n\nThese are required future tests and observable assertions, not results already\nexecuted. G01/C01 bind them to exact commands. Each chunk names its applicable\nIDs and adds feature-specific cases. Use fake transports/clocks/stores at real\nmodule interfaces plus literal external fixtures; native and live tests provide\nseparate evidence. Do not assert implementation-private structure for its own sake.\n\n## Core authority and SDK\n\n- **T01 Context isolation:** tenant/workspace/credential switch during a request\n  rejects old replies, retires pending reviews/one-time secrets, and cancels work.\n  Switching back cannot revive authority. Credential replacement and sign-out\n  behave the same way; duplicate completions settle at most once.\n- **T02 Review authority:** forged/stale/replayed/consumed review IDs, changed\n  draft/resource/context, model text, and imported history cannot Apply. Valid\n  operator Apply transmits the exact reviewed operation once.\n- **T03 Unknown outcomes:** disconnect before send, after send, timeout, cancel,\n  401/403/429/5xx, and partial multistep provisioning yield correct states. No\n  automatic mutation/scan replay; offer readback/review/explicit retry where valid.\n- **T04 SDK external contract:** independently authored request/response fixtures\n  verify method/path/body/headers/options, pagination/multipart limits, unknown\n  enum/action preservation, sanitized errors, and no dropped options.\n- **T05 Credential confinement:** scan-only setup works; management/inference/MCP\n  credentials are separate. Deny wrong-origin redirects or custom endpoint\n  changes that would forward a credential without an explicitly reviewed new\n  destination binding. Preserve legitimate operator-reviewed endpoint overrides\n  and explicitly selected nonsecret tenant-profile import; whitelist metadata,\n  discard embedded secrets and never follow credential-file references.\n  No implicit environment/tenant file\n  loading, upstream fallback, persistent renderer storage, or secret log fields.\n- **T06 Protected storage:** locked/denied/unavailable/corrupt store, interrupted\n  write/reset, session-only mode, restart, explicit one-time reveal/clipboard,\n  and cleanup. No plaintext fallback; v1 namespaces remain untouched.\n- **T07 Lifecycle:** bounded concurrent work, cancellation, queue backpressure,\n  superseding reads, app close/restart, sleep/wake, expired credentials, and\n  monotonic versus wall-clock decisions. UI remains responsive during work.\n\n## Presentation and Academy\n\n- **T08 Shared presentation:** web and desktop consumers build from the same\n  reviewed shared source; themes/fonts/routes render correctly. General desktop\n  screens mount no Academy gamification/account effects or web manager routes.\n- **T09 Native UI:** actual Tauri command path, menus/shortcuts, keyboard-only\n  workflow, focus restoration, VoiceOver labels/order, zoom/large text, themes,\n  reduced motion, long/empty/error content, and window resizing. Mock IPC tests\n  alone cannot establish these. macOS WebDriver availability must be verified;\n  use supported native accessibility/automation or recorded manual steps.\n- **T10 Academy identity:** device grant, pending/slow_down/expiry/cancel, refresh,\n  revocation, wrong issuer/client/scope, account switch/sign-out, and stale replies.\n  Token management stays Rust-owned. The learner account is independent of tenant.\n- **T11 Learning authority:** client bundles contain no answer keys/private lab\n  evaluation, privileged routes, or assessor tokens. Forged verdict/award/local\n  exam clock cannot pass; the hosted backend decides access, grading, and awards.\n  Signed-in denial differs from guest sign-in; sandbox never uses tenant secrets.\n- **T12 Offline work:** download lease/revision/account access, encrypted draft\n  save/restart, locked vault, sign-out/account switch, withdrawn content, duplicate\n  submission IDs, changed draft versions, and reconnection. No deferred action\n  replay. Valid local work remains usable without claiming offline grading.\n\n## Workflows and Harness\n\n- **T13 Collection semantics:** exact capability subsets, large collections,\n  bounded pagination/truncation, search/filter/page resets, stale/partial cache,\n  consistent Home/Inbox source facts, and no false complete/zero/absent claims.\n- **T14 API Client documents:** edit/save/conflict while typing, invalid input,\n  environment/secret separation, import/path traversal/size errors, multipart,\n  cookies/redirect auth, cancellation, history/download, Git/profile adoption,\n  and recovery from interrupted writes. Arbitrary workbench targets cannot borrow\n  privileged application credentials through renderer-supplied references.\n- **T15 Harness process:** actual native binary and bounded bootstrap/JSONL\n  protocol, malformed/oversized frames, correlation/duplicates, queue pressure,\n  crash/EOF/cancel, context change, graceful/forced cleanup, and missing/tampered\n  resources. No PATH launcher fallback or plaintext rollout/history.\n- **T16 Harness authority:** typed investigation/proposal tools, active-draft\n  protection, no engine Apply, one-time secrets absent from model context,\n  read-only project defaults, approved single-process command restrictions,\n  forbidden descendants/pipelines, stale decisions, and history replay.\n- **T17 Inference/MCP identity:** pinned Gateway origin; SSO PKCE/state/nonce/\n  issuer/client/audience/time/signature/JWKS/algorithm/key-rotation validation;\n  sleep/refresh/revoke/identity changes;\n  distinct inference/MCP grants; fixed conversation server scope; exact call-item\n  approvals; changed revision/grant; denied tools; trace/execution evidence.\n  No direct upstream provider fallback or global cross-workspace MCP authority.\n\n## Import, quality, and artifacts\n\n- **T18 Import transaction:** dry-run selection, versions/source lock, account\n  match, protected source reads, malformed/oversized/traversal inputs, interruption\n  at each commit point, restart, duplicate import, destination conflict, rollback,\n  and source-byte preservation. No credentials/sessions/approvals transfer.\n- **T19 Diagnostics privacy:** seeded canary secrets and customer content absent\n  from logs/crash files/exports by default; explicit redacted export; correct\n  retention/reset; no analytics/crash upload attempts; encrypted user history\n  remains a distinct intentional storage feature.\n- **T20 Performance:** named macOS hardware/OS/workloads, cold/warm startup, idle\n  and scan/large-list/streaming/Harness memory, main-thread responsiveness, file\n  and DMG footprint. Record methodology, repetitions, distribution and baseline;\n  set core budgets in C03 and register later workload budgets at the introducing\n  chunk's entry before implementation/acceptance measurement. See the budget\n  governance below; C24/C26 compare with pre-registered values.\n- **T21 Controlled live:** designated account/tenant/entitlements and disposable\n  resources, explicit operation allowlist/budgets, sanitized request IDs, readback,\n  cleanup receipts, deny/error cases, and unresolved outcome handling. Missing\n  access or unsupported operations remain blocked; no destructive production tests.\n- **T22 Installed package:** signed/notarized/stapled DMG, Gatekeeper/quarantine,\n  mount/drag/install/launch/relaunch with fresh user/home and no developer checkout,\n  PATH Node/Bun/managed CLI disabled, actual Rust sidecar, runtime inventory/hash\n  integrity, no hidden backend download, safe uninstall/reset/coexistence,\n  manual update, previous-version rollback, and preserved user work.\n- **T23 Cross-feature offline:** launch offline and read/edit each applicable\n  local category: saved cases, FP/FN reports, API Client documents, protected\n  Harness history, configuration drafts, and Academy work. Mark remote data as\n  stale/unknown as appropriate. Offline drafts do not retain valid Apply authority;\n  reconnect requires fresh readback/review when needed. Pending reviews, scans,\n  inference turns, generic HTTP mutations and MCP/tool calls never resubmit on\n  reconnect. Offer explicit retry while preserving uncertain remote outcomes.\n  Bind to C03,C09–C12,C14–C15,C17–C18,C20–C22 and cumulative C24.\n\n## Performance budget governance\n\nC03 registers numeric budgets and named methods for the shell/scan workloads\nbefore C04. Also register the later workload catalogue and owning chunks.\nBefore implementing/measuring a newly introduced workload, its owning chunk\ncommits a numeric budget, hardware/OS, dataset, measurement method and rationale:\nC11/C13 large collections; C15 large HTTP bodies/downloads; C18 streaming/charts;\nC19 native-engine startup/memory; C20 history; C21/C22 tool/MCP workloads.\nDo not wait until C24 to fit limits to observed results.\n\nA budget change requires a versioned rationale, original failures/results, and\nowner or independent reviewer acknowledgement recorded in the receipt. It cannot\nsilently erase a regression. Benchmark evidence records the pre-registered budget\nhash; the controller rejects retroactive or mismatched budget inputs. C24/C26\ncompare against those exact pre-committed values, including changed-budget history.\n\nNormal bounded metadata reads or credential refresh prerequisites for an explicit\noperation are not deferred business-action replay. Tests must distinguish these\nfrom silently resubmitting scans, writes, grading, or tool calls after reconnect.\n\nBefore review, challenge security-critical tests with a relevant deliberate\nbehavior mutation or equivalent negative probe. Show that violating an invariant\nfails the test; do not create an exhaustive mutation framework or mirror every\nimplementation detail. Run mutations only in isolated disposable copies.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
