{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_54","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/architecture/ui-v2/prototype/MANIFEST.sha256 && rg -n 'R2Harness|convo=|data-props|function Harness|convoHint|archiveActive|function startNewConvo' docs/architecture/ui-v2/prototype/R2Harness*.html | head -35 && sed -n '240,300p' docs/architecture/ui-v2/state-ownership.md && sed -n '78,98p' docs/architecture/ui-v2/state-ownership.md && sed -n '1710,1838p' docs/planning/decisions.md\"","aggregated_output":"ce90fce7789c485b67fac300ecfa81e831bf23bc26da8cca2b7ff3dd45712638  R2App.dc.html\n5f8c08d8c439c402b27bbf5eb896884966fb4f794c1c85fcd62c2d9f17268b1d  R2Harness.dc.html\nbdd59a833b9896c851021bc815cd61a21d88f21f345f3da96b2395f053275ccb  R2Harness2.dc.html\n0700f12e8b74c5c8d822bab01dec53c52eda60eb47dd79ecb93b3c8b7e156076  R2Harness3.dc.html\n2fec0565909fb6d1889655ebf3db1222f93a3a152edf6785e899e03a892c1eda  R2Onboard.dc.html\nafe2f2ef3ee68207a2d2487b43b89a4aeb3625f8ec792996a9a4da936485658b  R2Rationale.dc.html\ndocs/architecture/ui-v2/prototype/R2Harness2.dc.html:12:<dc-import name=\"R2App\" start=\"harness\" convo=\"mcp\" hint-size=\"1440px,900px\"></dc-import>\ndocs/architecture/ui-v2/prototype/R2Harness2.dc.html:15:<script type=\"text/x-dc\" data-dc-script data-props='{\"$preview\":{\"width\":1440,\"height\":900}}'>\ndocs/architecture/ui-v2/prototype/R2Harness3.dc.html:12:<dc-import name=\"R2App\" start=\"harness\" convo=\"keys\" hint-size=\"1440px,900px\"></dc-import>\ndocs/architecture/ui-v2/prototype/R2Harness3.dc.html:15:<script type=\"text/x-dc\" data-dc-script data-props='{\"$preview\":{\"width\":1440,\"height\":900}}'>\ndocs/architecture/ui-v2/prototype/R2Harness.dc.html:12:<dc-import name=\"R2App\" start=\"harness\" convo=\"blocks\" hint-size=\"1440px,900px\"></dc-import>\ndocs/architecture/ui-v2/prototype/R2Harness.dc.html:15:<script type=\"text/x-dc\" data-dc-script data-props='{\"$preview\":{\"width\":1440,\"height\":900}}'>\n| `ts.topicRev`, `ts.topicDesc`, `ts.injApplied`, `ts.aesApplied`, `ts.aesRev` | applied review results | Core | read back from the product scopes after apply | — | kept for Runtime topics; `aes*` **dropped** (P18) |\n| `ts.keyRotated`, `ts.keySecretClosed`, `ts.keyDeployed`, `ts.keyDoneAt` | rotation stage | Core | `Tenant.rotations` → `ShellSnapshot.rotations: RotationView{stage, oldKey, newKey, issuedBy, deploymentAcknowledgedAt, oldKeyRemovedAt, removalRejected}`; stage derived (issuanceUnconfirmed, issued, resumed, acknowledged, removalUnconfirmed, complete); intent `acknowledgeDeployment` | per tenant, persisted (timestamps stored once, and the reviewed key name that alone confirms an unconfirmed issuance); the held secret only on its change result, for its context | **changed** (P20, P41): supported Version → Delete sequence; see screens/reviews-administration.md |\n| `ts.follows` | follow-up status per conversation | Core | sessions | persisted | kept |\n| `ts.convo`, `ts.userConvos`, `ts.msgsBy`, `ts.decs`, `ts.ended`, `ts.archived` | current conversation, user conversations, messages, decisions, ended flags, archives | Core | existing harness sessions and decisions | persisted, protected | kept |\n| `ts.draft` | composer draft | UI | shell store per tenant and workspace ID (`harnessDrafts`), with kept messages (`harnessUnsent`: not sent, or not confirmed until their own conversation reports the core took their send ID, `ConversationView.submissions`) | transient | kept |\n\n## Credential entry paths (native)\n\n| Credential | Entry | Storage | Readable by the web layer |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Scan API key | webview password field read through a ref, cleared at once, sent once as `EnteredSecret` inside `configureScanCredential` (P25) | vault (`cr-` record), Keychain v2 namespace | state and mode only (`ScanView`), never the value |\n| Management client secret | same path, `configureManagementCredential` (optional section) | vault | client id and state only |\n| Gateway workspace key | same path, `configureInferenceCredential` | vault | state only |\n| Issued API key (rotation) | `RevealSecret` host sheet / host clipboard | held per result for the active context, never persisted; retired by the review's exit (`DiscardChange` on the applied review, also before the change settles), by acknowledging the deployment, or by a context change | never |\n\n## D048 — Harness desktop tools, project folder, approvals and proposals\n\nDecided in C21 (implementer decision for the C21 milestones, recorded for\nreview).\n\n**Desktop tools.** The engine is given one dynamic-tool namespace,\n`airs_desktop`, and nothing else of the desktop. Its tools are typed:\n- `inventory`, `detail`: the Runtime Security inventories C08 reads.\n  The DLP catalog is not offered.\n- `session`, `search`: C11's investigation reads. Session content is\n  already stripped by the management layer.\n- `redTeam`, `supplyChain`: C13's observation reads.\n- `scan`: one C03 scan with a profile named by the model, through the\n  tenant's own scan key.\n- `cases`: the C12 library's names and expectations.\n- `proposeChange`: stages a change for the operator to review (below).\n\nEvery call goes through the same admission as the operator's own reads:\n`admit_management`, `admit_scan` and the library. The call runs under the\nconversation's context and is refused once that context is stale. The\ndesktop holds the credentials. Results are JSON:\n- JSON carried as text is opened first: at most 4 layers deep and 4 MiB.\n  Text still encoded past those bounds is replaced by a note, never\n  passed on unchecked (P06, C21 reviews F2 and R2-F2);\n- field names that carry credentials are removed at any depth (14\n  names, case- and dash-insensitive);\n- the conversation's key is redacted;\n- a longer result becomes `{\"cut\": note, \"partial\": text}`, shortened\n  until the whole envelope, escaping included, fits 24 KiB (P06, C21\n  review R2-F4).\n\nA call is parsed into a closed set:\n- `deny_unknown_fields`;\n- arguments of at most 40 KiB, pages of at most 50 and scan text of at\n  most 16 KiB;\n- any credential-like argument name is refused.\n\nThe desktop runs a call only when it belongs to the turn being answered,\nand only once per call ID; a repeat is a protocol failure. Anything else\nis answered \"This tool or its arguments are unavailable.\" and nothing\nruns. At most 8 calls or approvals wait at a time. A call has 60 s, and\nthe engine's pending tool calls are dropped with the turn (interrupt or\nend). Each call carries its run and turn lease. When the turn ends, the\ncall is cancelled, and so is the runtime operation it admitted (P06, C21\nreviews F1 and R2-F1): queued work settles NotSent and sends nothing;\nwork already dispatched keeps its own outcome; no result is sent late. This follows C19's rule that a reply to a request the engine forgot\nis fatal.\n\n**Proposals, never Apply.** `proposeChange` accepts Runtime and DLP kinds\nonly, with create, update or delete. Gateway changes need the operator's\nown workspace review (C17). Before staging, the proposal passes the same\nstatic checks a review makes, through the tenant's management client\n(`changes::normalize`). Nothing is read or sent at that point.\n- A conversation holds at most 8 proposals, numbered `P-1`… The model is\n  told that nothing was applied.\n- Review opens the proposal with the operator's existing\n  `review_change`. It is refused (`Busy`) while any change review is\n  open, so the operator's own draft or review is never replaced.\n- Administration shows the opened review with the proposal's exact\n  draft, so Apply sends what was reviewed. Only the operator's\n  `RequestApply` and the native confirmation (C09) write.\n- A proposal reads `inReview` only while its review is the open one, then\n  `closed`. Dismissed proposals are hidden.\n\n**Project folder and access.** The operator chooses one folder per tenant\nin the native \"Choose project folder\" sheet. The folder is stored as\nnonsecret state with a write flag that is off by default. These folders\nare refused:\n- the home folder itself, and `.ssh`, `.aws`, `.gnupg`, `.config`, `.kube`,\n  `.docker`, `Library`, `.azure` and `.netrc` under it;\n- `/System`, `/usr`, `/bin`, `/sbin`, `/private/etc`, `/private/var/db`,\n  `/private/var/root`, `/Library`, `/Applications`, `/dev` and `/cores`;\n- this app's data root, and any folder containing it.\n\nThe folder and its access are part of the engine binding:\n- The bootstrap carries `projectPath` and `localAccess`\n  (`readOnly`/`workspaceWrite`).\n- The thread carries `cwd` and the `read-only` or `workspace-write`\n  sandbox.\n- Without a folder, the thread has no local environment.\n\nChanging the folder or its access retires the running conversation. A\nsaved conversation whose folder or access differs reads `otherProject` and\ncannot be continued: a new conversation is needed. Restoring a compatible\none restores no approval, and no command is run again.\n\n**Approvals.** The engine's command and file-change requests wait for the\noperator under the desktop's own token, 12 random bytes. A token is valid\nonly for that run and turn and is cleared at interrupt and at turn end, so\na stale or repeated decision is refused. A request is declined at once,\nwithout asking, when any of these holds:\n- there is no folder;\n- it is a file change while changes are off;\n- it asks for added permissions, network access or another root;\n- it belongs to another turn;\n- what it would do cannot be shown whole: a command longer than 8 KiB (or\n  none), or a file change whose every file is not named whole (none\n  named, a change without a path, more than 64 files or a path past\n  1 KiB) (P06, C21 reviews F3 and R2-F3: never shown cut or partial);\n- 8 decisions are already waiting.\n\nThe pinned engine's own desktop sandbox enforces the rest. The negative\nprobes are recorded:\n- one process, with exec replacement only, so children and pipelines fail\n  with \"fork: Operation not permitted\";\n- no network;\n- reads outside the folder fail with \"Operation not permitted\";\n- writes only inside the folder, and only with `workspaceWrite`.\n\nThe engine's own patch writer (`apply_patch`) names its files only with\nthe change item, which may reach the desktop before or after the approval\nrequest. Asked first, the request is declined at once; asked after, the\napproval shows the named files whole. Approved, the patch still fails to\nwrite in this build: it runs in the engine's narrower helper sandbox (the\nengine is not changed). Allowed shell commands are what change the folder.\nBoth orders are exercised (harness_tools, native C21-N04).\n\n**Renewal with changes allowed.** When a company sign-in renewal\n(D047) would restart an engine that may change the folder, the message is\nheld (`confirmWrite`). Allow renews, restarts and restores with the same\naccess, then sends. Not now gives the message back, and nothing is\nrestarted, restored or sent.\n\n**Out of scope here:** MCP servers and their token rotation (C22);\nimported history (C23); live management, Gateway and model qualification\n(C25).\n\n## D049 — Workspace MCP servers for the Harness: catalog, desktop OAuth, grants and approval\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
